---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CowTree from "../../components/CowTree.astro";
import MirrorHeal from "../../components/MirrorHeal.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("copy-on-write");
const L = {
  bp: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 374, 382),
  bpSize: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 361),
  cksum: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 381),
  dvas: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 362),
  compute: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 411, 417),
  passRewrite: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 128),
  allocStage: gh("include/sys/zio_impl.h", 153),
  hole: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 4345, 4346),
  metaslab: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 4380, 4382),
  hole0: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 2107, 2115),
  ddt: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 2126, 2131),
  nop: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 2133, 2136),
  writeDone: gh("module/zfs/dbuf.c", 5152, 5154),
  kill: gh("module/zfs/dsl_dataset.c", 294),
  dead: gh("module/zfs/dsl_dataset.c", 332, 345),
  childVerify: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 1631, 1639),
  label: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 175, 177),
  labelVerifier: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 295, 298),
  embedded: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 386, 393),
  gang: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 178, 180),
  zilog: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 181, 182),
  zilog2: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 190, 191),
  off: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 173, 174),
  fletcherDefault: gh("include/sys/zio.h", 119, 120),
  fletcher: gh("module/zcommon/zfs_fletcher.c", 317, 338),
  childSelect: gh("module/zfs/vdev_mirror.c", 793, 802),
  repairCond: gh("module/zfs/vdev_mirror.c", 883, 886),
  noGood: gh("module/zfs/vdev_mirror.c", 878, 881),
  counter: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 5295, 5300),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="copy-on-write"
  title="Copy-on-write, checksums and self-healing · Uberblok"
  description="Why a ZFS pool stays consistent without fsck, and how a checksum stored in the parent catches a disk that lies: interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          Disks return wrong data without reporting an error. A bit rots; a write lands at the wrong place, or never
          lands at all; a firmware bug answers with stale sectors. A checksum stored next to the data cannot catch a lost
          or misdirected write, because the stale block carries a checksum that matches it. And a filesystem that
          overwrites live blocks in place can be left half old and half new by a crash.
        </p>
        <p>ZFS answers both problems with one rule and one placement.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="never">
      <h2 id="never"><span class="n">1</span> Never overwrite a live block</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          When a block changes, ZFS writes the new version somewhere else and leaves the old one where it is. The
          pointer to the block changes, so its parent changes and is written elsewhere too, and so on up to the root.
          The old tree stays whole until a single write, the new uberblock, switches the pool from one tree to the
          other. Rewrite a block step by step, and cut the power before the commit.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CowTree lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="parent">
      <h2 id="parent"><span class="n">2</span> The checksum lives in the parent</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each block pointer, the <a href={L.bpSize}>128-byte</a> <a href={L.bp}><code>blkptr_t</code></a>, carries the
          <a href={L.cksum}>256-bit checksum</a> of the block it points to. A block never vouches for itself: its parent
          does, and the parent’s parent vouches for the parent, up to the uberblock. Reading a block means recomputing
          its checksum and comparing it with the one in the pointer that led to it. On a mirror, a mismatch sends the
          read to the other disk, and the good copy is written back over the bad one.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MirrorHeal lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          ZFS never overwrites live data and keeps each block’s checksum in the pointer to it, so a crash cannot leave
          the pool half written and a disk cannot return wrong data unnoticed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Every write goes to a new place: the block gets a new address, which changes its parent’s block pointer,
          which changes the parent, up to the root. Nothing the current uberblock reaches is ever modified, so at every
          instant the disks hold a complete, consistent tree, and writing one new uberblock commits the next one. Each
          block pointer also carries the checksum of its child, which makes the tree a Merkle tree: one trusted root
          vouches for every block below it. A mismatch on read is a detected error, never silent corruption, and
          with a second copy (a mirror, or <a href={L.dvas}>up to three addresses</a> in one block pointer) ZFS
          returns the good copy and repairs the bad one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <strong>Where a write becomes a new block.</strong> In the write pipeline,
            <code>zio_write_compress</code> decides between a rewrite in place and a fresh allocation.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/zio.c"
          from={2086}
          to={2105}
          symbol="zio_write_compress"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 2094,
              to: 2101,
              text: `In-place rewrite is allowed only for a block born in the very txg being synced (<code>BP_GET_BIRTH(bp) == zio-&gt;io_txg</code>), of unchanged size, from sync pass <a href="${L.passRewrite}"><code>zfs_sync_pass_rewrite</code></a> (2) on. No committed uberblock references such a block yet, so overwriting it touches no live data. This is the one exception to copy-on-write for blocks of the tree.`,
            },
            {
              from: 2102,
              to: 2104,
              text: `Every other write zeroes the block pointer and takes the full write pipeline, whose <a href="${L.allocStage}">DVA_ALLOCATE stage</a> normally gives the block a new address: <code>zio_dva_allocate</code> <a href="${L.hole}">asserts the pointer is a hole</a> before <a href="${L.metaslab}">calling <code>metaslab_alloc</code></a>. Just below, three cases change the pipeline: a block that compresses to nothing <a href="${L.hole0}">becomes a hole</a>, dedup <a href="${L.ddt}">takes the DDT pipeline</a>, and nopwrite <a href="${L.nop}">adds its own stage</a>.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            When the write completes, <a href={L.writeDone}><code>dbuf_write_done</code></a> calls
            <code>dsl_dataset_block_kill</code> on the old pointer and <code>dsl_dataset_block_born</code> on the new one.
            The old block is <a href={L.kill}>freed only if it was born after the most recent snapshot</a>; otherwise it
            <a href={L.dead}>goes on the dataset’s deadlist</a>, and the snapshot keeps it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where the checksum is checked.</strong> On write, <code>zio_checksum_compute</code>
            <a href={L.compute}>stores the computed value in <code>bp-&gt;blk_cksum</code></a>. On read, the block is
            checked against that field.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/zio_checksum.c"
          from={501}
          to={538}
          symbol="zio_checksum_error_impl"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 502,
              to: 505,
              text: "The expected value is <code>bp-&gt;blk_cksum</code>, taken from the block pointer the reader followed; the actual value is recomputed over the bytes just read.",
            },
            {
              from: 515,
              to: 526,
              text: "Encrypted blocks other than objsets: half of the 256 bits is the encryption MAC, which decryption verifies, so only the other 128 bits are compared here.",
            },
            {
              from: 535,
              to: 536,
              text: `A mismatch returns <code>ECKSUM</code>. On a mirror this runs in each child read, not in the parent: <code>zio_vdev_child_io</code> <a href="${L.childVerify}">moves the CHECKSUM_VERIFY stage down to the child</a>. Each error is <a href="${L.counter}">counted on the leaf vdev</a>.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The chain stops at the uberblock: <code>ub_rootbp</code> has no parent pointer. Each uberblock slot is
            protected instead by an <a href={L.label}>embedded SHA-256 “label” checksum</a> whose
            <a href={L.labelVerifier}>verifier is the slot’s own offset on disk</a>. <a href={L.gang}>Gang headers</a> and ZIL blocks
            (<a href={L.zilog}><code>zilog</code></a>, <a href={L.zilog2}><code>zilog2</code></a>) also
            <a href={L.embedded}>carry embedded checksums</a>, and <a href={L.off}><code>checksum=off</code></a> exists.
            The default, <code>checksum=on</code>, <a href={L.fletcherDefault}>means fletcher4</a>: four 64-bit running
            sums over the block <a href={L.fletcher}>read as 32-bit words</a>. The figures above compute it for real.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where the mirror heals.</strong> In <code>vdev_mirror_io_done</code>, if no child has returned good
            data yet, <a href={L.childSelect}><code>vdev_mirror_child_select</code> picks another child</a> and a new read
            is issued. If none is left, <a href={L.noGood}>the read fails with an error</a>. Repair needs at least one
            good copy, a writable pool, and either an unexpected error, a resilver, or a resilvering scrub
            (<a href={L.repairCond}>L883–L886</a>). Then:
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/vdev_mirror.c"
          from={900}
          to={934}
          symbol="vdev_mirror_io_done"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 900,
              to: 904,
              text: "Children that returned good data are never rewritten: the comment just above warns that rewriting the only good copy during a power loss could lose it.",
            },
            {
              from: 905,
              to: 924,
              text: "A child that was not tried is repaired only during a scrub, for indirect or dRAID-spare vdevs, or when its DTL says this txg is missing there.",
            },
            {
              from: 927,
              to: 933,
              text: "The good buffer <code>zio-&gt;io_abd</code> is written to each damaged child as a <code>ZIO_FLAG_IO_REPAIR</code> write, flagged <code>ZIO_FLAG_SELF_HEAL</code> when the cause was an unexpected error.",
            },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figures leave out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figures show one file, so the tree is short: in a pool it runs from the uberblock through the MOS, the
        dataset and the object’s dnode. They leave out sync passes, gang blocks, dedup, nopwrite, encryption MACs, and
        the mirror’s load-based choice of the side to read first (here, always disk A). The uberblock ring has 32
        slots, as on 4K-sector disks. Checksums are real fletcher4 values over the demo blocks; addresses and txg
        numbers are made up.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="copy-on-write" />
</Base>

